# Vulnerability details

## Impact
In the `Stable2.decodeWellData` function, there is a critical typo that could lead to the incorrect assignment of token decimals. The function intends to set a default of 18 decimals if the decoded value is 0, but due to a copy-paste error, it checks `decimal0` twice instead of checking `decimal1` in the second condition.

All operations involving the second token would be calculated as if it had 18 decimals, regardless of its actual decimal places.

## Proof of Concept

This error could cause significant issues in the following scenarios:
1. If `decimal0` is 0 and `decimal1` is 6 (or any value other than 0 or 18), the function will incorrectly set `decimal1` to 18.
2. This misrepresentation of token decimals will lead to severe miscalculations in all Well operations, including incorrect liquidity calculations when adding or removing liquidity and incorrect swap calculations.

## Tools Used

Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

```solidity
function decodeWellData(bytes memory data) public view virtual returns (uint256[] memory decimals) {
    (uint256 decimal0, uint256 decimal1) = abi.decode(data, (uint256, uint256));

    // if well data returns 0, assume 18 decimals.
    if (decimal0 == 0) {
        decimal0 = 18;
    }
    if (decimal1 == 0) { // <---------------------
        decimal1 = 18;
    }
    if (decimal0 > 18 || decimal1 > 18) revert InvalidTokenDecimals();

    decimals = new uint256[](2);
    decimals[0] = decimal0;
    decimals[1] = decimal1;
}
```


